How important to take into account are the following considerations for Sam when deciding what to do?')
  RETURNING id INTO v_s01;

  -- S02: Jehovah's Witness refusing blood transfusion — partner pressure
  VALUES ('A 38-year-old patient with a significant gastrointestinal bleed has stable vital signs but is at clear risk of deterioration without a blood transfusion. She is a practising Jehovah''s Witness, has full capacity, and has calmly and consistently stated she will not accept blood products under any circumstances. Her partner is in the room and is becoming distressed, repeatedly asking the F1 doctor, Tomas, to ''just give her the blood anyway'' if her condition worsens overnight.

How appropriate are each of the following responses by Tomas?')
  RETURNING id INTO v_s02;

  -- S03: Staff nurse publicly criticising F1 doctor
  VALUES ('While shadowing on a busy surgical ward, Eli, a fourth-year medical student, witnesses a senior staff nurse loudly criticise an F1 doctor in front of patients and other staff for taking too long to write a discharge summary. The nurse uses a mocking tone, and the F1 leaves the desk visibly upset. Several patients are watching from their beds, and other staff have lowered their eyes and continued working.

How appropriate are each of the following responses by Eli?')
  RETURNING id INTO v_s03;

  -- S04: Patient requesting unjustified sickness certificate
  VALUES ('During a GP rotation, junior doctor Ahmed sees a regular patient who asks for a two-week sickness certificate to cover an upcoming work deadline he says is ''killing him.'' The patient describes feeling stressed but, on questioning, has no symptoms that meet the threshold for clinical sickness absence and is otherwise well. He mentions that his previous GP ''always sorted this out for him'' without much fuss.

How appropriate are each of the following responses by Ahmed?')
  RETURNING id INTO v_s04;

  -- S05: Peer reference for unprofessional flatmate
  VALUES ('Approached at the end of an outreach placement, Aisha, a final-year dental student, is asked by her flatmate Theo — also a final-year dental student — to provide a positive peer reference for a competitive academic prize application. Aisha has been concerned about Theo''s lack of professionalism on placements: he has been routinely late, dismissive towards dental nurses, and on one occasion was overheard joking about a patient. He has been a kind flatmate to her personally and is unaware of her reservations.

How important to take into account are the following considerations for Aisha when deciding how to respond?')
  RETURNING id INTO v_s05;

  -- S06: Consultant making discriminatory remark about patient's family
  VALUES ('After an outpatient clinic in respiratory medicine, Yusuf, an F1 doctor, is in the office with a senior consultant who is finishing notes. As the consultant scrolls through the next day''s list, he comments to Yusuf, in a low voice, that he ''always finds Mrs Achebe''s family difficult — they ask too many questions, must be a cultural thing.'' Yusuf has not met the family and feels uncomfortable with the remark.

How appropriate are each of the following responses by Yusuf?')
  RETURNING id INTO v_s06;

  -- S07: Clinical mentor inviting student for one-to-one drink
  VALUES ('After a particularly long teaching session, Maya, a fourth-year medical student, receives a private message from her clinical mentor, a registrar in his thirties, suggesting they ''meet for a drink, just the two of us, to talk about your career.'' Maya has previously enjoyed his teaching and feels he has been supportive, but the message makes her uncomfortable. He is responsible for assessing her end-of-placement competency in two weeks'' time.

How important to take into account are the following considerations for Maya when deciding how to respond?')
  RETURNING id INTO v_s07;

  -- S08: Patient with dementia trying to leave ward
  VALUES ('On a busy elderly care ward, Sasha, an F1 doctor, is paged because Mr Hopkins, an 84-year-old patient with moderate dementia, has been found near the lift in his hospital gown trying to leave the ward. He is not detained under any legal framework and has previously been assessed as lacking capacity to make decisions about discharge. He is calm but insists he wants to ''go home.'' The afternoon ward is short-staffed.

How appropriate are each of the following responses by Sasha?')
  RETURNING id INTO v_s08;

  -- S09: Medical student at roadside emergency
  VALUES ('Driving home after an evening shift, Tara, a final-year medical student, comes across a single-vehicle road traffic collision on a quiet country road. There is one casualty, a young man, conscious but bleeding from a head wound and slumped against the wheel. There is no other passing traffic visible, the ambulance has been called, and the estimated arrival time is 15 minutes. Tara has basic first aid training but has never managed an unsupervised emergency.

How important to take into account are the following considerations for Tara when deciding how to respond?')
  RETURNING id INTO v_s09;

  -- S10: Clinical colleague sharing identifiable case on social media
  VALUES ('Foundation year one doctor Zara notices that a fellow F1 has posted about a patient from their shared ward on a publicly visible social media account. The post describes the patient''s unusual diagnosis and includes the approximate age, the ward name, and enough clinical detail that the patient could potentially be identified. The post has already attracted several comments from the F1''s followers.

How appropriate are each of the following responses by Zara?')
  RETURNING id INTO v_s10;

END $$;

